:: HelpWithTools [childhood]
# Learning by Helping

"Can I help make something?" you ask the hunter, eager to try your hand at crafting.

The hunter considers for a moment, then nods. "You can help me prepare sinew for binding spear points," he says, selecting a dried animal tendon from his collection of materials.

He shows you how the dried sinew must be pounded gently with a smooth stone to separate the fibers, then moistened slightly to make it pliable. "When it dries again after being wrapped around the spear point, it will shrink and create a very tight bond," he explains.

Your small fingers are actually well-suited for the delicate work of separating the sinew fibers. The hunter guides you, showing you how to apply just the right amount of pressure without damaging the material.

"Good binding is as important as a sharp point," he tells you. "If the binding fails during a hunt, the point may come loose at the worst possible moment."

As you work, he explains how different materials serve different purposes in toolmaking - stone for cutting edges, wood for handles and shafts, sinew and plant fibers for binding, resins for sealing and waterproofing.

When you've finished preparing a length of sinew, the hunter uses it to bind a small stone point to a thin shaft, creating a dart for hunting birds or small animals. "This binding was prepared by <<print $character.name>>," he announces, showing the finished tool to another hunter nearby.

